  "text": "Solution 1 is correct, as far as I can tell from memory. I could not check the literature, so this rests on recalled results rather than a verified source.\n\n- **Degree ≤ 4 suffices.** I recall Braun and Oréfice-Okamoto (2016) proving that a nowhere-vanishing Jacobian plus deg p ≤ 4 forces global injectivity. The earlier cases (deg ≤ 2, deg ≤ 3 by Braun and dos Santos Filho) are consistent with this.\n- **Degree 5 fails.** Solution 1 says there are non-injective examples with deg p = 5 (Braun–Fernandes). I have a vague recollection of such a construction, but I can't confirm it. If that holds, the answer is 4.\n- **Solution 2 is less plausible.** It claims deg p ≤ 5 forces injectivity, which contradicts the degree-5 counterexample above. It also cites a degree-6 counterexample, which is not needed if the degree-5 one exists. Its degree-5 positive result is the claim I trust least.\n\nSince the question asks for the sharp bound, and the sharpness claim is the part I'm least sure of, the answer 4 is my best judgment, not a certainty.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
